              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "**altavista.com** was the web address of **AltaVista**, one of the most popular and pioneering search engines of the early internet era. \n\nHere is a quick look at its history, legacy, and what happened to it:\n\n### 1. The Origins (1995)\nAltaVista was launched on December 15, 1995, by researchers at **Digital Equipment Corporation (DEC)**, including Paul Flaherty, Louis Monier, and Michael Burrows. Interestingly, it wasn't initially designed to be a commercial search engine product; it was created as a showcase project to demonstrate the raw processing power of DEC's new 64-bit Alpha computers.\n\n### 2. Why It Was Revolutionary\nBefore AltaVista, searching the web was slow, and directories like Yahoo! required websites to be manually submitted and categorized. AltaVista changed the game with several major innovations:\n* **The First Full-Text Index:** It was the first search engine that crawled and indexed the full text of web pages, rather than just titles or descriptions.\n* **High-Speed Crawler:** Its web crawler, named \"Scooter,\" could crawl millions of pages much faster than its competitors.\n* **Natural Language Queries:** It allowed users to type search queries in natural, conversational language rather than complex boolean syntax.\n* **Babel Fish:** In 1997, AltaVista launched the web's first real-time translation service, \"Babel Fish,\" which translated words and entire websites into multiple languages.\n\n### 3. The Peak and The Pivot\nBy 1996, AltaVista was handling tens of millions of search queries per day and was the go-to search engine for early web surfers. \n\nHowever, corporate shifts crippled its momentum. When Compaq acquired DEC in 1998, they attempted to turn AltaVista into a \"web portal\" (similar to Yahoo! or AOL). They bloated the homepage with news, weather, shopping, and email, which alienated users who just wanted a clean, fast search bar. \n\nMeanwhile, a small startup named **Google** launched in 1998 with a minimalist homepage, advanced page-ranking algorithms, and a singular focus on search. Google quickly began pulling users away.\n\n### 4. Acquisition and Closure\nAs its market share plummeted, AltaVista changed hands multiple times:\n* **1999:** Compaq sold a majority stake in AltaVista to CMGI.\n* **2003:** It was bought by **Overture Services**, which was subsequently bought by **Yahoo!** later that same year.\n* **The End:** Yahoo! kept AltaVista running in the background for a decade. On **July 8, 2013**, Yahoo! officially shut down AltaVista.\n\n### Where is it now?\nIf you type `altavista.com` into your browser today, the URL simply redirects to **Yahoo! Search**. While the physical site is gone, AltaVista is remembered as the giant that laid the technical foundation for how modern search engines operate.",
